| position | command | info lines | deepest | bestmove | elapsed s |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| startpos | `go infinite` | 30 | (30, 'cp', 7, 93981191) | after stop: d2d4 | 75.0 |
| startpos | `go` | 30 | (30, 'cp', 7, 93981191) | after stop: d2d4 | 75.0 |
| drawn threefold | `go infinite` | 64 | (64, 'cp', 0, 0) | after stop: d5d1 | 75.0 |
| drawn threefold | `go` | 64 | (64, 'cp', 0, 0) | d5d1 | 0.0 |

RESULT — and it REFUTES the generalisation in 23-F189
------------------------------------------------------
| position | command | info lines | deepest reached | bestmove | elapsed |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **startpos** | `go infinite` | 30 | **depth 30** (cp 7, 93,981,191 nodes) | only after `stop`: d2d4 | **75 s (my cap)** |
| **startpos** | `go` | 30 | depth 30 | only after `stop`: d2d4 | 75 s (my cap) |
| drawn threefold | `go infinite` | 64 | depth 64 (cp 0, 0 nodes) | d5d1 | **0.0 s** |
| drawn threefold | `go` | 64 | depth 64 | d5d1 | 0.0 s |

**On startpos, `go infinite` did NOT terminate at depth 64 — it ran for my full 75-second cap and had reached only
depth 30, and needed `stop` to return a move.** So 23-F189's sentence "go infinite stops at the depth ceiling in this
build, immediately on a drawn root and after ~19 s on a normal position" is TRUE for the sparse K+R v K twin it was
measured on and FALSE for a dense position.

The correct statement:
- the `max_depth <= 64` clamp is real (23-F187) and BINDS on sparse positions, where 64 is reachable in seconds;
- on a dense position 64 is far out of reach, so `go infinite` behaves as one expects — it runs until `stop`, and the
  clamp never comes into play;
- **the ~19 s figure was the K+R v K bare twin exhausting depth 64, NOT "a normal position"** — I described a sparse
  endgame as if it were representative, which is the same error as every other one-fixture generalisation in this seat.

For a harness author the load-bearing fact is therefore: `go infinite` is bounded by `stop` (or 64, whichever comes
first), and on a real position it is `stop` that matters.
